  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSurface high pressure continues to build in from the north for \neastern New York and western New England today. The Frost/Freeze\nprogram has now ended, today September 21st, for the southern \nAdirondacks. For more information on the Frost/Freeze program \nacross eastern New York and western New England, please visit \nweather.gov/media/aly/ALY_Frost_Freeze_Fall2026.pdf or \nweather.gov/aly and click the first headline called Fall 2026 \nFrost Freeze Program. \n\nThrough Wednesday, dry conditions and cooler temperatures are in \nstore, especially for high terrain locations. Low temperatures \nacross eastern New York and western New England could observe the \nnext two nights in the upper 30s (high terrain locations) to the mid \n40s (river valley locations). These low temperatures range between \n10 to 15 degrees below normal. The high temperatures across eastern \nNew York and western New England through Wednesday range in the 50s \nfor higher terrain locations and in the mid-60s in the valleys. This \nis 5 to 10 degrees below normal for the middle of September. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nForecast Confidence: Low\n\nMeteorological Overview: Latest ensemble forecast model \nguidances continue to fluctuate with the weekend coastal low \npressure system, but they are all in agreement that a surface \nlow pressure system develops off the Atlantic Coast and moves \nnortheastward for the end of the work week. Thursday is shaping \nup to be dry and seasonable for temperatures as the surface high\npressure system moves eastward. Now, there are multiple \nscenarios that could occur with the exact track of the center of\nthe low. The most supported scenario by the latest forecast is \nfor low chances of light rain showers Friday through the weekend\nas the surface low stays west, but close enough to the coast to\nbring these low chances and no significant impacts. The surface\nlow could meander just off of the southern New England coast, \nbringing these low chances for rain showers across western New \nEngland and portions of eastern New York through the entire \nweekend. The second scenario is the low pressure system stays \nwell out to sea and surface high pressure continues. The third \nscenario is that the low remains close to the coast where gusty \nwinds develop and medium to high chances of light rain showers. \nThe key takeaway for Friday night into Saturday is that for the \neastern Catskills, Mid-Hudson Valley, Taconics, Berkshires, \nLitchfield Hills, wind gusts are forecasted to range between 25 \nand 40 mph. There are no concerns for flooding across eastern \nNew York and western New England. \n\nImpact: Breezy conditions Friday and Saturday for locations \nsouth of Albany, especially across the eastern Catskills, \nTaconics, Litchfield Hills, and Berkshires, could knock over and\nmove loose outdoor objects.",
